{
  "teamId": 145,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Chicago White Sox were a team in the middle of a deliberate rebuild, and their 62-100 record reflected that reality honestly. Opening with a perfect 2-0 in March, the Sox generated a flicker of hope before April swallowed them whole — a 6-18 month defined by bullpen meltdowns, walk-off heartbreaks, and a young roster still learning how to lose gracefully. The one bright spot was Matt Davidson, whose home run power provided genuine electricity in a sea of early-season disappointment.\n\nMay and June continued the struggle, as the pitching staff — particularly the relief corps featuring Nate Jones, Joakim Soria, and Luis Avilán — repeatedly surrendered leads in crushing fashion. The 8-19 May and 12-17 June records told the story of a team talented enough to compete at moments but not yet consistent enough to win series. Young pieces like Yoán Moncada were flashing their ceiling, but the floor kept giving way beneath them.\n\nThe true revelation of the season arrived in August, when the White Sox posted a surprising 17-12 record — their only winning month and a genuine proof of concept for the rebuild. Daniel Palka emerged as an unexpected power threat, José Abreu continued his steady production, and the team played with an energy that hinted at the future. It was a month that gave the fanbase something real to hold onto amid a hundred losses.\n\nSeptember brought the curtain down predictably, a 8-19 collapse that mirrored April's misery as younger arms wore down and the wins dried up. Yet through it all, players like Tim Anderson and Daniel Palka delivered memorable moments that pointed toward better days. The 2018 White Sox were not a good team, but scattered across their difficult season were enough promising performances to suggest the rebuild was moving in the right direction.",
